        "σ O33-H34 -> lp O33"
      ],
      "routePreference": "preferred",
      "explanationAfterStep": "A proton transfer gives the neutral Criegee intermediate, a peroxy ester on a tetrahedral carbon."
    },
    {
      "stepKey": "migration",
      "stepKind": "polar",
      "toStateId": "protonated-lactone",
      "fromStateId": "criegee",
      "canonicalArrows": [
        "σ O32-O33 -> lp O32",
        "σ C2-C7 -> σ C7-O33",
        "lp O15 -> π C2-O15"
      ],
      "routePreference": "preferred",
      "explanationAfterStep": "The key step. The weak O–O bond breaks and 3-chlorobenzoate leaves; at the same time the bridgehead carbon moves from the ring carbon to the oxygen, inserting an oxygen into the ring. The bridgehead CH migrates rather than the CH₂ because it is more substituted. It moves with retention of configuration, so the bicyclic shape, and the stereochemistry Corey set in the Diels–Alder step, survive (not checked by the app yet)."
    },
    {
      "stepKey": "deprotonation",
      "stepKind": "polar",
      "toStateId": "products",
      "fromStateId": "protonated-lactone",
      "canonicalArrows": [
        "lp O32 -> σ O32-H34",
        "σ O15-H34 -> lp O15"
      ],
      "routePreference": "preferred",
      "explanationAfterStep": "The carboxylate takes the proton, giving the neutral lactone and 3-chlorobenzoic acid."
    }
